;; reader.corpus -- reader-layer gap ledger (metadata, syntax-quote
;; qualification, auto-resolved keywords/namespaced maps, number
;; literals, char/string escapes, tagged literals, validation).
;; Every form here currently DIVERGES between real Clojure 1.13.0-alpha6
;; (reader.golden) and mova (reader.mova, target/release/mova).
;; Forms that turned out to already conform were moved to
;; tests/conformance/corpus/reader.corpus instead -- see that file.


;; metadata: S5/M3 built the subsystem (src/value.rs's `Value::Meta`,
;; src/reader.rs's `read_meta`, src/builtins/meta.rs), so all but one of
;; the rows that used to live here now conform and were promoted to
;; corpus/metadata.corpus. What is LEFT is the `:tag` resolution
;; question: real Clojure's compiler resolves a bare `^Tag` symbol to a
;; java.lang.Class (`(meta ^String [])` is `{:tag java.lang.String}`),
;; while mova deliberately leaves it the unresolved symbol `String` --
;; there is no JVM class world here to resolve into, and resolving would
;; turn every type hint in existing source into an unresolved-symbol
;; error. See `Interp::eval_meta_form`'s doc for the full trade.
(meta ^String [])

;; syntax-quote: substitution (~/~@) and, as of W3e-1, namespace
;; qualification of free symbols both conform now -- those rows moved to
;; the main corpus. What is left is NESTED syntax-quote: mova's
;; `quasiquote` has no depth tracking at all (see src/eval/quasiquote.rs's
;; module doc), so an inner backtick is not re-quoted and an inner
;; `~`/`~@` fires at the wrong level. The inner `quasiquote` head is
;; itself a symbol in the template, so W3e-1 now qualifies it too and the
;; mova column reads `(clojure.core/quasiquote user/a)` where it used to
;; read `(quasiquote a)` -- still the same one gap (no depth tracking),
;; just spelled with the qualification the rest of the reader now does.
``a

;; namespaced map literals (`#:ns{...}`/`#::{...}`/`#::alias{...}`) now
;; READ correctly: SPEC-W6b taught the reader
;; `LispReader.readNamespacedMap`, reusing `::`'s own `NsContext` for the
;; two auto-resolved spellings, and the fourteen forms that prove it live
;; in tests/conformance/corpus/ns-map-literal.corpus (two of them promoted
;; straight out of this file).
;;
;; What is left below is the PRINTER, and it is not really about
;; namespaced maps at all: `*print-namespace-maps*` defaults to TRUE under
;; `clojure.main` and to FALSE in mova, so the oracle prints
;; `#:foo{:a 1}` where mova prints `{:foo/a 1}` -- each the correct
;; rendering of the SAME map under its host's own default. mova does print
;; `#:foo{:a 1}` once the var is set (verified). Promoting these two means
;; changing that default, which is a decision about every printed map in
;; the language, not a reader fix.
#:foo{:a 1}
#::{:a 1}

;; number literals mova's reader cannot parse at all (falls through to
;; "invalid number literal"): ratios, BigInt/BigDecimal suffixes, radix
;; notation, and the ##Inf/##-Inf/##NaN symbolic literals.

;; char/string escapes mova's reader doesn't implement: \oNNN octal char
;; literals, and \NNN (octal)/\b/\f string escapes (src/reader.rs
;; read_string only handles \n \t \r \\ \").
"\b"
"\f"

;; tagged literals: ANY unregistered tag (`#foo/bar`) is accepted and its
;; payload passed through unchanged, where real Clojure throws (no
;; data-reader registered for that tag). `#uuid` left this bucket in S6
;; (assert/namespace/uuid batch) and `#inst` left it in SPEC-W1 task 4 --
;; both now read as real values and conform; see
;; tests/conformance/corpus/reader.corpus's own `#uuid`/`#inst` lines.
(read-string "#foo/bar 1")

;; validation Clojure's reader performs that mova's doesn't: nested #(...)
;; forms and malformed `::`/`:::` keywords are both silently accepted by
;; mova instead of being rejected.
#(#(%))
::
